A map gives three views to loop over: `keySet()` for keys, `values()` for values, and `entrySet()` for pairs. Each pair is a `Map.Entry<K, V>` with `getKey()` and `getValue()`, so you skip a second lookup:

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Map<String, Integer> counts = new TreeMap<>(Map.of("the", 3, "cat", 1, "and", 2));
        {{code}}
        System.out.println(counts);
    }
}
```

```java
for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : counts.entrySet()) {
    String key = entry.getKey();
    int count = entry.getValue();
}
```

A `HashMap` keeps no useful order. It can even change when the map grows. Looping over it directly is fine when order cannot matter, such as a total or a count of repeats. To print a listing, copy the keys into a list and sort it:

