}

steps.body:
A `LinkedHashMap` keeps keys in insertion order: the order they were first put. Updating an existing key does not move it. Use it when the output should follow the input, and a `TreeMap` when it should be sorted.

```java
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;

Map<String, Integer> seen = new LinkedHashMap<>();
seen.put("pear", 1);
seen.put("apple", 2);
System.out.println(seen); // {pear=1, apple=2}
```

`putIfAbsent(key, value)` stores the value only when the key is missing, and leaves an existing value alone. That makes grouping easy: make sure each key has a list, then add to it.

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        String team = "red";
        String player = "Ann";
        {{code}}
        System.out.println(byTeam);
    }
}
```

```java
Map<String, List<String>> byTeam = new LinkedHashMap<>();
byTeam.putIfAbsent(team, new ArrayList<>());
byTeam.get(team).add(player);
```

`get(team)` returns the list stored in the map, not a copy, so `add` changes the map's list directly. Printing a `Map` of `List` values shows `{red=[Ann, Bo], blue=[Cy]}`.

Gotcha: `new ArrayList<>()` is built on every call, even when the key already has a list. That waste is small here. The Lambdas lesson shows `computeIfAbsent`, which only builds the list when it is needed.

